    "title": "Murfreesboro Pike tree-planting project will add nearly 500 trees to benefit Nashville - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Elisheva Wimberly ,\n39 minutes ago\nNASHVILLE, Tenn. (WKRN) — Metro Water Services is coordinating a new tree-planting project.\nThe project calls for planting nearly 500 trees along Murfreesboro Pike to Old Murfreesboro Road. The Metro Water Services Department routinely plants trees along the streetscape.\nNeighborhood News: Stories impacting your community | Read More Metro Water Services Urban Forestry Coordinator Mike Jameson said the city is investing in more trees to help with city management. Trees absorb an enormous amount of storm water, bring shade, improve air quality and provide beautification to streetscapes in neighborhoods.\n“It’s great for the city; trees do an enormous number of wonderful things from the city management perspective,” Jameson said. “It’s one of the best investments you can have. Every dollar we spend on a tree, we save $3.“\nJameson said Murfreesboro Pike has some areas significantly lacking tree canopy, and the project is best for the longevity of the city. He said the area only has 17% of canopy coverage, and as a result, the asphalt is brutally affected by the sun.\n⏩ Read today’s top stories on wkrn.com\n“It takes a lot of work,” Jameson said. “We’ve been working on this for a few months. We have to check each individual site for underground, utilities, traffic, compliance. We’re sending written notice to all of the property owners and soliciting any questions they may have, but once we get them in, we’ll have the same positive effects we’ve seen through the city of Nashville along the other street that we planted.”\nThe tree planting season in Middle Tennessee runs roughly from Nov. 1 to the end of March, which is when the trees themselves tend to be more dormant. Jameson said they’ll begin planting the trees toward the end of this year and early next year.",
